From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
Low-cost, low-power SoC microcontrollers with Bluetooth
and Wi-Fi
ESP32
[68]
[69] Image: image

ESP-WROOM-32 module with ESP32-D0WDQ6 chip
Manufacturer | [70]Espressif Systems
Type | Microcontroller
Released | September 6, 2016 (2016-09-06)[1]
[71]CPU | 
* [72]Tensilica Xtensa LX6
* Tensilica Xtensa LX7
* [73]RISC-V
Memory | 520 KiB SRAM
Power | 3.3 V DC
Predecessor | [74]ESP8266
ESP32 is a family of low-cost, energy-efficient
[75]microcontrollers that support both [76]Wi-Fi and
[77]Bluetooth. These chips feature several processing
options, including the [78]Tensilica Xtensa LX6
microprocessor available in dual-core and single-core
variants, the Xtensa LX7 dual-core processor, or a
[79]RISC-V microprocessor. The ESP32 incorporates
components for wireless data communication such as
built-in antenna switches, an RF [80]balun, power
amplifiers, low-noise receivers, filters, and
power-management modules.
Typically, the ESP32 is embedded on device-specific
[81]printed circuit boards (PCBs) or offered as part of
development kits that include a variety of [82]GPIO pins
and connectors, with configurations varying by model and
manufacturer. The ESP32 was designed by [83]Espressif
Systems and is manufactured by [84]TSMC using their 40 nm
process.[2] It is a successor to the [85]ESP8266
microcontroller.
= Features
[[86]edit]
[87]
[88] Image: image
ESP32 [89]die shot. Most of the chip area is covered by a
[90]power distribution network on the top metal layers.
Since the release of the original ESP32, a number of
variants have been introduced and announced. They form the
ESP32 family of microcontrollers.[3] These chips have
different CPUs and capabilities, but all share the same
SDK and are largely code-compatible. Additionally, the
original ESP32 was revised (see ESP32 ECO V3, for
example). 
